---
// Five-multiplier deep dive — the information gain competitors' tool pages
// don’t provide. Rendered statically so search engines crawl every word.
---

<section id="formula" class="page-shell section-pad scroll-mt-20 border-t border-[var(--line)]">
  <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[var(--text)]">How Genshin damage is actually calculated</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-[var(--muted)]">
    Every non-transformative hit is one multiplication of seven factors. If you understand each one, you can
    diagnose exactly why a hit deals the number it does — and which single upgrade will move it the most. The
    calculator above breaks your exact panel into these same factors. The math follows the official damage formula,
    cross-checked against the
    <a href="https://genshin-impact.fandom.com/wiki/Damage" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-forest-600 underline decoration-forest-500/50 underline-offset-2 hover:text-forest-700">Genshin Impact wiki damage formula</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="panel mt-8 overflow-x-auto p-6">
    <p class="tnum whitespace-nowrap text-center text-sm text-[var(--muted)] md:text-base">
      <span class="text-forest-600">Total ATK</span>
      <span class="mx-2">×</span> Skill Multiplier
      <span class="mx-2">×</span> (1 + <span class="text-hydro">DMG Bonus</span>)
      <span class="mx-2">×</span> <span class="text-cryo">CRIT</span>
      <span class="mx-2">×</span> <span class="text-pyro">Reaction</span>
      <span class="mx-2">×</span> <span class="text-dendro">DEF</span>
      <span class="mx-2">×</span> <span class="text-electro">RES</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <details class="accordion mt-8">
    <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-[var(--surface)] p-5 font-semibold text-[var(--text)] transition-colors hover:border-forest-300">
      <span>How each of the 7 multipliers works</span>
      <svg class="h-5 w-5 shrink-0 text-[var(--muted)] transition-transform group-open:rotate-45" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" /></svg>
    </summary>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-6">
    <!-- 1. ATK -->
    <div class="panel p-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">1. Total ATK <span class="text-forest-600">— the base of everything</span></h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        Total ATK is built from three parts: <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">Base ATK</strong> (your character’s
        white number plus your weapon’s base attack — <em>not</em> the flat attack from artifacts), multiplied by
        <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">ATK%</strong> bonuses (artifact sands, sub-stats, weapon passives, team
        buffs), then plus <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">Flat ATK</strong> (the feather artifact’s +311, and
        Bennett’s buff). The formula is:
      </p>
      <p class="tnum mt-3 rounded-[10px] bg-[var(--surface-2)] p-4 text-center text-sm text-[var(--text)]">
        Total ATK = (Base ATK) × (1 + ATK%) + Flat ATK
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        Worked example: a level-90 character with 800 base ATK, a 46.6% ATK sands, 20% from sub-stats, and a +311
        feather has <span class="tnum text-[var(--text)]">800 × 1.666 + 311 ≈ 1,644 ATK</span>. The key insight is
        that <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">ATK% only multiplies Base ATK</strong> — so on a low-base-ATK character
        like Hu Tao (106 base ATK), ATK% is nearly worthless and HP or Elemental Mastery scales far better.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 2. Skill multiplier -->
    <div class="panel p-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">2. Skill multiplier <span class="text-forest-600">— your talent number</span></h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        Every talent has a multiplier expressed as a percentage of ATK — a "300%" talent means the hit deals 3.0× your
        Total ATK before any other factor. This is the single most underrated upgrade in the game: each talent level
        from 8 to 10 is a guaranteed <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">6-7% damage increase</strong> on that skill,
        with no RNG, no farming, no resin lottery. Leveling your main talent to 9 or 10 is almost always the highest
        return-per-resin action you can take, which is why the calculator defaults to a talent-10 reference panel.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        When comparing characters, remember that a character’s power is the product of their talent multipliers
        <em>and</em> how often those hits land. A 900% single hit (Zhongli’s Planet Befall) and a 200% hit that
        repeats twenty times (Ayaka’s burst) can deal similar total damage.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 3. DMG Bonus -->
    <div class="panel p-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">3. DMG Bonus <span class="text-hydro">— elemental &amp; weapon bonuses</span></h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        Your elemental goblet (46.6%), weapon passives, artifact set bonuses (Crimson Witch’s Pyro bonus, Emblem’s
        burst bonus), and supports like Kazuha or Yelan all add into <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">one single
        multiplier</strong>: (1 + total DMG Bonus). Because they stack additively, the more DMG Bonus you already
        have, the less each additional 46.6% is worth <em>relatively</em>. This is exactly why an ATK% goblet can beat
        an elemental goblet when a character already has a huge DMG Bonus from their kit.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        The general rule: if your DMG Bonus (goblet + weapon + set) is under ~100%, an elemental goblet is almost
        always best. Past ~150% total bonus, a well-rolled ATK% goblet becomes competitive. The calculator’s
        "Add +46.6% DMG bonus" advisor row shows exactly how much an elemental goblet is worth on your current build.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 4. CRIT -->
    <div class="panel p-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">4. CRIT <span class="text-cryo">— the 1:2 balancing act</span></h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        A crit multiplies the hit by (1 + Crit DMG). Averaged over many hits, your expected damage uses the multiplier
        <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">(1 + Crit Rate × Crit DMG)</strong>. Because Crit Rate and Crit DMG multiply
        each other, and because a single Crit Rate roll (max 3.9%) is worth exactly half a Crit DMG roll (max 7.8%),
        the mathematically optimal balance is <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">1:2</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        Concretely: a 50% / 200% build has a crit multiplier of 1 + 0.5 × 2.0 = <span class="tnum text-[var(--text)]">2.0</span>,
        while a 70% / 160% build with the same total investment reaches 1 + 0.7 × 1.6 =
        <span class="tnum text-[var(--text)]">2.12</span> — a genuine ~6% damage gain just from rebalancing. Aim for at
        least 60% Crit Rate on a main DPS before stacking more Crit DMG.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 5. Reaction -->
    <div class="panel p-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">5. Reaction <span class="text-pyro">— Vaporize &amp; Melt amplify</span></h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        Vaporize multiplies the entire hit by 1.5×, Melt by 2.0× (when the correct element triggers). Elemental
        Mastery then scales that bonus by <span class="tnum text-[var(--text)]">2.78 × EM ÷ (EM + 1400)</span>, so the
        first 100 EM is worth far more than the next 100 — this steep drop-off is why reaction carries want
        <em>some</em> EM (roughly 100-200) but rarely stack it past 300. Transformative reactions (Overload, Bloom,
        Hyperbloom) work differently and are covered in the next section.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 6. DEF -->
    <div class="panel p-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">6. DEF multiplier <span class="text-dendro">— why level matters</span></h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        Enemy defense reduces your damage by
        <span class="tnum text-[var(--text)]">(Character Level + 100) ÷ ((Character Level + 100) + (Enemy Level + 100) × (1 − DEF Shred))</span>.
        Against a same-level enemy this is exactly 0.5 — you deal half your pre-defense damage. Against a level-100
        enemy it drops to ~0.487, which is why a level-80 carry loses noticeable damage against high-level Abyss
        floors. DEF Shred (Lisa, Klee C2, Ayaka C4, Raiden C2) directly raises this term and is one of the rarest,
        highest-value buffs in the game.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 7. RES -->
    <div class="panel p-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">7. RES multiplier <span class="text-electro">— shred beats stats</span></h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        Most enemies have 10% resistance, so the baseline multiplier is 0.9. The Viridescent Venerer 4-piece set
        shreds 40% RES, dropping a 10% enemy to −30%, which flips the multiplier to 1.15 — a
        <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">~28% damage increase</strong> from a single support. That is why an Anemo
        unit holding VV is in nearly every reaction team: no artifact sub-stat roll can match it. Below 0 RES each
        point of shred is worth half, so extra shred past 0 has diminishing returns.
      </p>
    </div>
    </details>

    <!-- Why it matters -->
    <div class="panel mt-6 border-forest-500/30 bg-forest-500/8 p-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-forest-600">Why this matters: multiplying, not adding</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        Because all seven factors <em>multiply</em>, raising your weakest factor is worth far more than pushing an
        already-high factor even higher. If your Crit multiplier is 1.3 while your DMG Bonus is already 1.8, one more
        Crit roll beats another DMG bonus every time. The calculator’s damage breakdown highlights your weakest
        multiplier in red — start there. This is the entire philosophy behind the "highest-value upgrades" list:
        it re-simulates each change and tells you which one actually moves the number.
      </p>
    </div>
</section>
